WEBVTT


00:00:01.250 --> 00:00:04.880
A knitter was using a hand knitting
pattern that instructed her to work

00:00:04.970 --> 00:00:08.990
left-leaning and right-leaning
double decreases.

00:00:10.060 --> 00:00:14.650
Now, hand knitting instructions for this
type of decrease can be worded in a lot of

00:00:14.650 --> 00:00:15.610
different ways.

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:21.310
Let's look at how to create these
decreases on the knitting machine.

00:00:23.099 --> 00:00:25.740
This is a right-leaning, double decrease,.

00:00:27.090 --> 00:00:30.360
A double decrease, just means
you're decreasing two stitches.

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:36.989
In this example, there are three stitches
that are knit plain along the edge of the

00:00:36.989 --> 00:00:37.699
knitting.

00:00:38.730 --> 00:00:41.160
Allowing these three stitches to roll.

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:45.060
This creates a neat
no-finish knitted edge.

00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:51.780
The direction of the lean of the
decreases provides a decorative addition.

00:00:53.809 --> 00:00:59.460
Notice that these stitches are leaning to
the right as you view the knitting from

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:00.769
the knit side

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:07.479
to work this on the machine, we need to
think a little bit backwards because we're

00:01:07.479 --> 00:01:09.759
looking at the purl side of the knitting.

00:01:10.630 --> 00:01:15.460
To work a right-leaning, double
decrease, leaving three stitches plain.

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:18.770
You'll transfer two stitches to the right,

00:01:26.630 --> 00:01:33.590
putting three stitches on one needle and
then transfer the three plain stitches to

00:01:33.590 --> 00:01:35.510
fill in those empty needles.

00:01:40.055 --> 00:01:43.955
And be sure to take the decrease
needles to out of work position.

00:01:45.815 --> 00:01:51.335
This is a left-leaning, double decrease,
and you'll see this more commonly.

00:01:52.255 --> 00:01:57.475
Again, we're decreasing two stitches along
the edge of our knitting, leaving three

00:01:57.475 --> 00:01:58.615
plain stitches.

00:01:59.804 --> 00:02:02.535
Notice that the stitches are leaning left.

00:02:02.535 --> 00:02:05.714
As you view the knitting from
the knit side of the work.

00:02:07.395 --> 00:02:12.704
To work this on the machine, you'll
transfer two stitches to the left, putting

00:02:12.704 --> 00:02:18.704
three stitches on the needle, then fill in
the emptied needles to complete the double

00:02:18.704 --> 00:02:19.394
decrease.

00:02:32.125 --> 00:02:33.805
Let's look at this again.

00:02:33.985 --> 00:02:41.425
Each time you have a stack of three
stitches, the key is the first stitch in

00:02:41.425 --> 00:02:42.115
the stack.

00:02:42.775 --> 00:02:47.515
In other words, pay attention to the
stitch that we'll be showing on the public

00:02:47.515 --> 00:02:48.595
side of the knitting.

